## Terraform in one sentence
Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code tool created and maintained by HashiCorp that uses declarative configuration files to define the desired infrastructure state, then creates and updates that infrastructure consistently.

## Declarative configuration and the .tf files
With Terraform, you write configuration that describes what you want, rather than writing an imperative, step-by-step script for how to reach it. Terraform configuration files commonly use the .tf extension, and they focus on infrastructure resources such as compute, networking, storage, and DNS. This declarative approach helps you keep changes repeatable and reviewable, which is especially useful compared to making one-off changes through cloud consoles.

## The core workflow: configuration to apply
A typical workflow starts by writing your configuration, then initializing providers, then generating an execution plan, and finally applying the approved changes. Terraform can work with multiple providers through a provider plugin model, which is how it connects to external services and APIs. Before Terraform makes changes, it builds an execution plan that shows what will be added, changed, or removed, so you can review the impact.

## Why Terraform state matters
Terraform tracks managed infrastructure state so it can detect drift and changes, meaning it can notice when real infrastructure no longer matches what your configuration declares. State is central because Terraform uses it to remember what it manages and to compare the declared configuration with the actual infrastructure.

## Minimal example and commands
Here is a minimal example that creates a simple storage bucket using a provider, which illustrates the idea of declaring a desired resource in configuration.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "example" {
  bucket = "my-example-bucket-12345"
}
```

After writing configuration, you can run a short command sequence to initialize providers, view the proposed changes, and then apply them.

```bash
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply
```

This workflow supports repeatable, version-controlled infrastructure provisioning across many cloud services and providers.

Vue’s core programming model is component-based, meaning you build your UI from reusable units rather than one large page script. A Vue component combines a template for markup, script logic for state and behavior, and optional styles into a single unit you can reuse across an app. This structure helps you keep UI, behavior, and presentation together so changes stay easier to reason about.

Inside a component, the template uses HTML-like syntax with Vue-specific bindings that connect data to the DOM. For example, you can bind text to state so the displayed value follows the current data, and you can use directives to control what appears. Directives such as v-if and v-for let you render conditionally and render lists, which is especially useful for dashboards, menus, and repeated UI elements.

Reactivity is the engine that keeps everything in sync, because data changes are reactive and Vue updates the UI when state changes. Computed properties derive values from reactive state and cache results, which means you can express “derived UI data” clearly without manually recalculating it everywhere. Methods handle actions and event responses inside a component, so user interactions can trigger state updates that automatically refresh the rendered output.

Vue also standardizes common interaction patterns in templates, including event handling and two-way binding. Event handling is commonly done with v-on or the shorthand @, and two-way binding for form inputs is commonly expressed with v-model. For parent-child communication, props pass data from parent components to child components, and emits let child components communicate events upward to parents.

Here is a minimal single-file component that shows a template, props, a computed property, and reactive rendering. 
```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <p>Greeting: {{ greeting }}</p>
    <p>Uppercase: {{ upperGreeting }}</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  props: {
    greeting: String
  },
  computed: {
    upperGreeting() {
      return this.greeting.toUpperCase();
    }
  }
};
</script>
```
This example demonstrates how a prop flows into the component and how a computed property updates the UI when the reactive input changes.
